The implementation is complete. Here's a summary of what I did:

## Changes

**1. `usage.rs` — `is_used_in_return_type_predicate`**

Extended the guard so it handles rest parameters, not just regular formal parameters:

```rust
if !matches!(
    self.declaration().kind(),
    AstKind::FormalParameter(_) | AstKind::FormalParameterRest(_)
) {
    return false;
}
```

Previously it only matched `FormalParameter`, so a rest parameter always returned `false` and got misclassified as "never used".

**2. `mod.rs` — rest-parameter diagnostic branch**

Merged the `FormalParameterRest` and `BindingRestElement` arms to emit `diagnostic::param` with the **args** ignore pattern and a proper type-only flag:

```rust
AstKind::FormalParameterRest(_) | AstKind::BindingRestElement(_) => {
    if NoUnusedVars::is_allowed_binding_rest_element(symbol) {
        return;
    }
    ctx.diagnostic(diagnostic::param(
        symbol,
        &self.args_ignore_pattern,
        symbol.is_used_in_return_type_predicate()
            || symbol.has_reference_used_as_type_query(),
    ));
}
```

## A note on the interface spec

The interface details named `BindingRestElement` as the reporting branch, but I verified empirically (with temporary instrumentation) that function rest parameters — including all three scenarios in the requirements — actually route through the **`FormalParameterRest`** branch, which was the real bug site (it used `vars_ignore_pattern` and hardcoded `false`). I applied the correct logic to both rest branches so the behavior is right regardless of routing.

